Second viewpoint analysis - Contrasting perspective with equal depth  
Societal viewpoint.

While the business viewpoint looks at who earns the from a profit persepective, the societal viewpoint asks who is affected and how. It judges the devlopment in computing by how it impacts communities, consumers, workers and real people who add a change in this world, rather than analysing the revenue or how much the market share has changed. For big corperations like ebay and amazon, this means looking beyond things of that nature and instead keeping turning to pages about trust, oppotunities, jobs created or even lost and the inequalities or qualities that these online marketplaces have therefore created. 

Trust and community
The earliest importance for Ebay was socail as much as it was commercial. Their system allowed the use of trading items via online with no use of meeting up in person, replacing the use of a physical shop. This model proved very valuable to Ebay with Resnick et al. (2006) found that consumers would pay 7.6% more for items from established cooperations than from newcomers. Your reputaion became a source of social currency with Ebays users forming communities that benefited both parties, exchanging advice and goods. Amazon later learned and devloped this idea through customer reviews. This shifted the narriative from consumers trusting a shopkeeper to trusting the public reception and the crowd. However, the negative in this is that this trust is only as realible as the reviwes behind it, which can be manipulated with anyone having the power to write a review on any item.

Access and opportunity
The barriers to entry for individuals and small firms have been lowered from the growth and presence by online marketplaces. According to reports from Amazon, third party sellers, with most of them being both small and medium sized sellers, sold 4.1 billion products accounting for %60 of its sales in 2022 (PYMNTS, 2023). 37.5% of enterprises had in Ireland had had e-commerece sales in in 2025, including 34.9% of small enterprises. (CSO, 2025). It is also stated directly fro Amazon themselves that sellers in rual areas grey by more than %40 year after year. This is an important figure, howeveer is it also importent to acknowledge is a self reported figure and should be treated with caution. Consumers gained the power of gaining wider choice and the easier to acess the price comparison of items with the click of a finger regardless of their geographical location, which particularry benefits people who live in very remote areas and with little to no shops near. 
As good as the benefits may seem, unfortunatly, they are uneven. Sellers have no real power in the market as they depend on platforms that are able to set the median fee, rules and search visibility by constructing the algorithim to their own will, so a sellers power in the market is merley an iullusion. Local retialers set up are often priced out and face competition from firms who have no shop. People who live without a realibale broadband or online payment cards are also excluded from any sort of benefit the majjority have. The convience of one if often not felt by the other. 

Work and labour
The ability to complete same day delivery depends on human effort. In 2024, a US Senate Help commitee investigation found that over the course of seven years workers at Amazon warehouses were nearly twice as likely to be injured as the main blame from the workers was the feel of working in an extremley fast and and often dangerous pace (NPR 2024). These finidings were swiftly rejected by Amazon as they defended their methodology. Weather if true or false, this case shows the expectation of consumers of speed can transfer from the customer to the worker. It has also shows the social shift from socity to what people consider normal. Next day or instant delivery is no longer considered a luxary or a premiumum experience but has now turned into an assumption
